    contentHtml: `<section id="how-are-rattan-and-bamboo-different" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">How Are Rattan and Bamboo Different?</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Rattan isn’t a type of bamboo. Rattan is a solid-core climbing palm, so it’s flexible and bendable, while bamboo is a woody grass with hollow stems, so it’s stiff and keeps a straight shape.</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">This table compares the two materials on six points.</p></p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#e8ded1] shadow-2xs">
  <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm">
    <thead>
      <tr class="bg-[#14365d] text-white">
        <th class="p-3.5 font-semibold"><p class="c1"><strong>Feature</strong></p></th>
        <th class="p-3.5 font-semibold"><p class="c1"><strong>Rattan</strong></p></th>
        <th class="p-3.5 font-semibold"><p class="c1"><strong>Bamboo</strong></p></th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="divide-y divide-[#e8ded1] bg-white">
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c1">Plant type and structure</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Climbing palm, about 600 species, solid core</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Woody grass, more than 1,400 species, hollow, jointed stems</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c1">Growth</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Even diameter of 2-5 cm (3/4-2 in), climbs up to hundreds of meters, grows only in the tropics</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Each stem reaches full size in 3-4 months, grows from the tropics to temperate zones</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c1">Flexibility</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Bends by hand or around a mold after about 10-15 minutes of steaming</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Stiff as a whole pole and cracks under sharp bends, must be split into strips to weave</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c1">Strength</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Resilient, shock-resistant, doesn’t splinter</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">As strong as good timber, suited to straight, load-bearing structures</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c1">Moisture and outdoor use</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Dislikes long-term damp, best indoors or under cover</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Breaks down quickly with rain or ground contact, use it the same way as rattan</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c1">Cost and availability</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Usually pricier: mostly wild-harvested and handworked</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Usually cheaper: grown at scale and fast-growing</p></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-[#2d3748] mb-4">
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Why rattan bends and bamboo doesn’t:</strong> rattan is solid, with fiber bundles running continuously along its length, so once steamed soft it curves by hand or around a mold without breaking and holds its shape as it cools (1). Bamboo is hollow, so forcing a whole pole into a tight bend tends to split the stem wall lengthwise. Bamboo can still be woven, but it has to be split into thin strips first, the way bamboo baskets, blinds, and mats are made.</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Why rattan costs more than bamboo:</strong> almost all rattan is still harvested from the wild (3), much of it in Indonesia and other Southeast Asian countries, and then steamed, bent, and woven by hand. Bamboo is both cultivated and harvested from the wild, grows quickly, and can be harvested after a few years, so it’s usually cheaper as a raw pole.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section id="common-uses-of-rattan-and-bamboo" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">Common Uses of Rattan and Bamboo</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Rattan is usually used for pieces with curves and woven surfaces, while bamboo is used for straight structures and flat surfaces.</p></p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-[#2d3748] mb-4">
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Rattan:</strong> suits pieces that need curves and woven surfaces, such as armchairs, dining chairs, headboards, and tables with bent legs. The rattan skin is woven into cane chair seats and cabinet doors, cane panels let air through, which makes them a good fit for wardrobes (4). The rattan core is woven into baskets and wicker pieces. In decor, rattan often becomes pendant shades, mirror frames, and trays.</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Bamboo:</strong> suits straight structures and flat surfaces, such as shelves, straight-framed tables and chairs, pressed bamboo flooring, and wall panels, pressed bamboo is used for flooring because of its high hardness (2). Bamboo also becomes blinds, shades, and woven strips for baskets and mats. In the kitchen, it turns into chopsticks, cutting boards, and steamers.</li>
</ul>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Wicker isn’t a material but a weaving technique. Both rattan and bamboo can be woven wicker-style, but most wicker furniture is still made from rattan, especially round reed from the rattan core. The difference between rattan and wicker is explained in our rattan vs wicker guide.</p></p>
</section>

<section id="rattan-or-bamboo-which-should-you-choose" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">Rattan or Bamboo: Which Should You Choose?</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Choose based on the shape and job of the piece: rattan for curved woven pieces that need to handle knocks, bamboo for straight structures and flat surfaces.</p></p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-[#2d3748] mb-4">
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Choose rattan when:</strong> you need chairs, baskets, lampshades, or woven surfaces with curves, the piece will be sat on and leaned against often and needs resilience, and you’re fine paying more for handmade work.</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Choose bamboo when:</strong> you need straight posts, shelves, flooring, blinds, or panels, the piece has to carry loads along its length, and you want a material that’s easy to find and more affordable.</li>
</ul>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Whichever you choose, keep natural rattan and bamboo furniture indoors or under cover. If a piece has to stay outside year-round, synthetic rattan is the safer choice.</p></p>
</section>

<section id="rattan-vs-bamboo-faqs" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">Rattan vs Bamboo FAQs</h2>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">Is Rattan Stronger Than Bamboo?</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">It depends on what kind of strength you need. Bamboo is stiffer and handles compression better, so it suits posts, scaffolding, and flooring. Rattan is more resilient: it takes knocks and repeated sitting and leaning without splintering, so it suits chair frames and woven surfaces.</p></p>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">Is Bamboo Stronger Than Steel?</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">No. The often-quoted figure comes from tests on one species, Bambusa tulda, which reached a tensile strength of 60,000 psi (400 MPa). In general, bamboo is about as strong as a good softwood or hardwood (2), not a substitute for steel.</p></p>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">What Is the Difference Between Rattan and Willow?</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Rattan is a tropical climbing palm, while willow is a temperate tree whose flexible young shoots are used for weaving. Willow is the traditional wicker material in Europe, while rattan is more common in today’s wicker furniture.</p></p>
</section>

<section id="references" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">References</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">(1) Sika-Design. (n.d.). <em>What is rattan?</em> Retrieved November 10, 2026, from https://www.sika-design.com/pages/what-is-rattan</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">(2) Wikipedia contributors. (2026). Bamboo. In <em>Wikipedia</em>. Retrieved November 10, 2026, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamboo</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">(3) Wikipedia contributors. (2026). Rattan. In <em>Wikipedia</em>. Retrieved November 10, 2026, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rattan</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">(4) DeFrancesco, T. (2024, April 2). <em>The difference between cane, rattan, bamboo and wicker</em>. HH Perkins. https://hhperkins.com/blogs/news/the-difference-between-cane-rattan-bamboo-and-wicker</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1 c12"></p></p>
</section>`
