    contentHtml: `<section id="what-is-jute" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">What Is Jute?</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Jute is a long, glossy bast fiber harvested from the inner bark of plants in the genus Corchorus, which belongs to the mallow family (Malvaceae). It’s the second most produced plant fiber in the world, behind only cotton.</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Jute fiber comes from two main species: white jute (Corchorus capsularis) and tossa jute (Corchorus olitorius). Sources disagree on which one gives the better fiber: one reference notes that tossa jute is the main source while yielding fiber inferior to white jute, yet elsewhere describes tossa fiber as softer, silkier, and stronger. In Bangladesh, people call jute the "golden fiber" because of its color and economic value. Tossa jute leaves are edible as well: cooks in Egypt simmer them into a soup called molokhiya, while in Vietnam, the leaves often go into a soup with crab.</p></p>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">A Brief History of Jute</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">People were making textiles from jute as early as the Indus Valley civilization, around the 3rd millennium BC. In 1590, the Ain-e-Akbari recorded that poor villagers in India wore clothing woven from jute, while Bengalis had long relied on white jute ropes in everyday life (1).</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">British traders started dealing in jute in the 17th century. Early on, the fiber was so coarse that it had to be processed by hand, until someone in Dundee, Scotland, discovered that treating it with whale oil made it workable on machines. By 1895, Bengal’s jute industry had overtaken Scotland’s, and during World War I, Bengal shipped more than a billion jute sandbags to the trenches (1). The industry saw a steep downturn around 1970 as synthetic fibers took over, but it has rebounded as more countries ban single-use plastics (1).</p></p>
</section>

<section id="key-properties-of-jute" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">Key Properties of Jute</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Jute is a long, strong, breathable plant fiber made mainly of cellulose and lignin.</p></p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-[#2d3748] mb-4">
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Composition:</strong> jute fibers come from the bast (phloem) of the stem and consist mainly of cellulose and lignin, individual fibers range from 1 to 4 meters (3.3 to 13.1 ft) long and vary from off-white to brown (1).</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Two fiber grades:</strong> coarse fibers (jute butts) are made into sacking, hessian cloth, rope, and carpet backing, while finer fibers are woven into rugs, curtains, upholstery, and espadrilles (1).</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Texture:</strong> jute has a silky luster, though its softness varies by grade. Some sources describe jute as soft while others call it rough (1): finer fibers make softer fabric, while coarse fibers feel rougher.</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Strength, breathability, and insulation:</strong> jute is strong, breathable, and anti-static, and it insulates well against heat and sound, which is why it’s widely used in home textiles (1).</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section id="advantages-and-disadvantages-of-jute" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">Advantages and Disadvantages of Jute</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Jute is a good choice for items that need to be durable, breathable, and affordable, such as packaging, rugs, and decorative fabrics, but it’s poorly suited to clothing worn against the skin or to damp spaces.</p></p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#e8ded1] shadow-2xs">
  <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm">
    <thead>
      <tr class="bg-[#14365d] text-white">
        <th class="p-3.5 font-semibold"><p class="c1"><strong>Advantages</strong></p></th>
        <th class="p-3.5 font-semibold"><p class="c1"><strong>Disadvantages</strong></p></th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="divide-y divide-[#e8ded1] bg-white">
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c1">Strong and hard-wearing</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Sensitive to moisture: it can mildew and weaken when wet for long</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c1">Low cost and widely produced</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Coarse and scratchy against bare skin</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c1">Biodegradable and renewable</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Sheds fibers, especially when a rug is new</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c1">Rain-fed, with little need for fertilizer or pesticides</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Hard to wash with water, it can shrink and discolor</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c1">Breathable, anti-static, and sound- and heat-insulating</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c1">Biodegradable only with conditions: dyed jute or jute with rubber or latex backing won’t fully break down</p></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Sources also differ on how jute responds to sunlight. Some guides list long sun exposure as a drawback because it weakens the fiber, while another reference names colorfastness and UV protection among the reasons jute is used in home textiles. A practical reading is that jute handles indoor light well but shouldn’t stay outdoors in sun and rain for long periods.</p></p>
</section>

<section id="how-is-jute-made" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">How Is Jute Made?</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Jute fiber is made in four main stages: growing, retting, stripping, and spinning.</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1"><strong>Step 1: Growing.</strong> Jute thrives in alluvial soil and warm, humid monsoon climates with temperatures of 20 to 40 °C (68 to 104 °F), 70 to 80% relative humidity, and 5 to 8 cm (2 to 3 inches) of rain a week (1). India and Bangladesh lead global supply: in 2020, the two countries produced about 97% of the world’s jute, far more than the often-cited "over 80%" figure (1).</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1"><strong>Step 2: Retting.</strong> After harvest, farmers bundle the stalks and soak them in slow-moving water for about 10 to 30 days, so bacteria can break down the outer tissue and loosen the fibers.</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1"><strong>Step 3: Stripping, washing, and drying.</strong> Workers scrape away the non-fibrous material, pull the fibers free from the stem, then wash them and leave them to dry in the sun (1).</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1"><strong>Step 4: Grading and spinning.</strong> The fibers are sorted into coarse and fine grades, then spun into thread or heavier yarn for weaving fabric, twisting rope, or making rugs.</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1"><em>[Image: Bundled jute stalks retting in water beside bundles of dried golden jute fiber. Alt: "Jute stalks retting in water next to dried golden jute fiber"]</em></p></p>
</section>

<section id="what-is-jute-used-for" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">What Is Jute Used For?</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Jute is used across five main areas: interiors and decor, fashion, cars, agriculture, and packaging and rope.</p></p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-[#2d3748] mb-4">
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Interiors and decor:</strong> area rugs and runners, basket-style pendant shades, woven poufs and footstools, and macramé plant hangers. Decorators favor jute for its golden sheen and raw texture, which warm up minimalist spaces, it holds up to high-traffic floors and helps absorb sound. Jute is one of many natural fibers used in the home, our natural materials guide covers the other fiber groups.</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Fashion and accessories:</strong> espadrille soles, reusable tote bags, and clutches woven from jute blended with cotton or silk (1). Jute is breathable and rigid enough to hold the shape of a bag or shoe sole without plastic stiffeners.</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Cars:</strong> some carmakers use jute composites in door linings, the backing behind dashboards, and trunk liners (1). These composites weigh less than fiberglass or standard plastics and don’t shatter into sharp pieces in a collision.</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Agriculture and landscaping:</strong> jute netting laid across slopes and riverbanks to control erosion, along with mesh that wraps the root balls of transplanted saplings (1). The netting keeps the soil moist and breaks down once plants take root, so there’s nothing to clean up.</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Packaging and rope:</strong> burlap or hessian sacks for coffee, cocoa, grain, and sugar, along with wine bottle carriers, reusable shopping bags, and twine and rope twisted from coarse jute fiber. Jute is food-safe and breathable, so coffee beans don’t sweat the way they would in plastic. Demand for jute packaging is growing again as more countries ban single-use plastics (1).</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section id="jute-faqs" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">Jute FAQs</h2>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">Is Jute the Same as Burlap?</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Not exactly. Jute is the plant fiber, while burlap is the loosely woven fabric made from it (known as hessian in the UK). So traditional burlap is made of jute, but not every jute product is burlap, as jute rugs and jute rope show.</p></p>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">What Is Jute Called in English and Other Languages?</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">In English, people call the fiber jute, while fabric woven from it goes by burlap, hessian, or gunny, in the trade, unprocessed fiber is called raw jute (1). In Bengal, jute is known as paat, in Chinese it’s 黄麻 (huángmá), and in Vietnamese it’s đay.</p></p>
</section>

<section id="conclusion" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">Conclusion</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">Jute is a natural bast fiber from the Corchorus plant, grown mainly in India and Bangladesh and second only to cotton in plant fiber production. Its strength, breathability, low cost, and biodegradability make it a great fit for packaging, rugs, and home furnishings, though it dislikes persistent moisture and feels rough against the skin. When you choose jute products, go for fine fibers in rugs and fabrics, coarse fibers in packaging and rope, and keep them somewhere dry.</p></p>
</section>

<section id="references" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">References</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1">(1) Wikipedia contributors. (2026). Jute. In <em>Wikipedia</em>. Retrieved November 11, 2026, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jute</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c1 c9"></p></p>
</section>`
