    contentHtml: `<section id="how-are-seagrass-and-jute-different" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">How Are Seagrass and Jute Different?</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">Seagrass rugs have a smooth, slightly glossy, firmer waxy texture and a naturally stain-repellent surface, while jute rugs are softer but less durable, tend to shed fibers over time, and are sensitive to moisture.</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">This table compares the two rugs on seven points.</p></p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#e8ded1] shadow-2xs">
  <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm">
    <thead>
      <tr class="bg-[#14365d] text-white">
        <th class="p-3.5 font-semibold"><p class="c0"><strong>Feature</strong></p></th>
        <th class="p-3.5 font-semibold"><p class="c0"><strong>Seagrass</strong></p></th>
        <th class="p-3.5 font-semibold"><p class="c0"><strong>Jute</strong></p></th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="divide-y divide-[#e8ded1] bg-white">
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c0">Source and origin</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">A grassy plant grown in flooded areas, dried, then woven</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">A bast fiber from the plant stalks of Corchorus, grown mainly in India and Bangladesh</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c0">Feel and texture</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Smooth, slightly glossy, firmer waxy texture, can feel rigid underfoot</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">One of the softest natural plant fibers, plush and comfortable under bare feet</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c0">Color and dyeing</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Starts green and naturally weathers into a golden-brown color, can’t really be dyed</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Beige, golden, or brown with a more fibrous look, takes dye well</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c0">Durability</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Highly resilient, sheds little, suits high-traffic areas</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Softer but less durable, tends to shed fibers over time</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c0">Spills and moisture</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Non-porous, water-resistant surface wipes clean, but the rug must stay dry</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Sensitive to moisture, absorbs spills and stains easily, and can leave rings or warp</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c0">Maintenance</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Low-maintenance with regular vacuuming, spot-clean with very little water</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Regular vacuuming, blot spills right away</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c0">Price</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Sources disagree: some rank it the cheapest natural fiber, others the most expensive</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Usually among the more affordable natural fiber rugs</p></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">The main differences come down to feel and how each fiber reacts to liquids. Seagrass has a non-porous, waxy surface, so it repels stains but can’t really be dyed, while jute takes dye well but absorbs spills quickly. </p></p>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">Is Seagrass Really Grown Underwater?</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">Not really. Many guides describe seagrass as a plant grown underwater in shallow Asian wetlands, with a natural waxy coating that comes from salt water (1). In practice, according to a seagrass research organization, many products sold as "seagrass" are woven from a sedge (genus Cyperus) grown in Southeast Asia, and some are actually made from water hyacinth, a freshwater plant. True seagrasses are flowering plants that live in the sea, unlike the sedges grown in flooded fields. It’s also worth remembering that both are natural fibers, but that doesn’t make either rug automatically more eco-friendly, since cultivation, processing, backing, dyes, transport, and the rug’s lifespan all play a part.</p></p>
</section>

<section id="seagrass-or-jute-which-rug-is-right-for-your-" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">Seagrass or Jute: Which Rug Is Right for Your Room?</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">Choose seagrass for dry, high-traffic rooms that need a tough, low-maintenance surface, and choose jute for dry, low- to medium-traffic rooms where comfort takes priority over heavy wear.</p></p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#e8ded1] shadow-2xs">
  <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm">
    <thead>
      <tr class="bg-[#14365d] text-white">
        <th class="p-3.5 font-semibold"><p class="c0"><strong>Room</strong></p></th>
        <th class="p-3.5 font-semibold"><p class="c0"><strong>Seagrass</strong></p></th>
        <th class="p-3.5 font-semibold"><p class="c0"><strong>Jute</strong></p></th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="divide-y divide-[#e8ded1] bg-white">
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c0">Living room or family room</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">A great fit for busy living spaces</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">A great fit for formal sitting rooms where softness matters</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c0">Bedroom</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Works, but feels firmer underfoot</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Usually more comfortable under bare feet</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c0">Dining room</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">A good fit with a low, tight weave</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Works if you blot spills right away</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c0">Entryway or hallway</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Works well for high-traffic areas, depending on the weave</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Best kept to low-traffic spots</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c0">Bathroom</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Avoid unless the maker says otherwise</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Avoid</p></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="hover:bg-[#faf8f5]/60 transition-colors">
        <td class="p-3.5 font-medium text-[#0c2340] align-top"><p class="c0">Outdoors</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Not recommended unless the product is made for outdoor use</p></td>
        <td class="p-3.5 text-[#2d3748] align-top"><p class="c0">Generally unsuitable</p></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">Keep two things in mind before you buy. First, stain-repellent doesn’t mean moisture-proof: even though the plant grows in wet conditions, a finished woven rug with a backing still needs to stay dry to avoid mildew and water rings, so neither rug suits a bathroom or outdoor exposure. Second, the fiber is only part of the story, weight, thickness, weave, edging, and especially the backing also decide how well a rug holds up.</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">If toughness and low-maintenance cleaning matter most for a dry dining room or living room, choose seagrass, if softness matters most for a bedroom or reading nook, choose jute.</p></p>
</section>

<section id="seagrass-vs-jute-faqs" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">Seagrass vs Jute FAQs</h2>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">Is Seagrass a Good Material for a Rug?</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">Yes, as long as the rug stays in a dry room. Seagrass rugs are highly resilient, shed little, and have a stain-repellent surface that makes spills easy to wipe up. The trade-offs are that the fiber feels firm underfoot, can’t really be dyed, and a new rug may give off a faint hay scent for a while.</p></p>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">What Are the Downsides of Jute Rugs?</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">Jute rugs are softer but less durable than sisal or seagrass, tend to shed fibers over time, and absorb spills quickly, which can leave water rings or warp the weave. That’s why jute rugs work best in dry, low- to medium-traffic rooms. </p></p>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">Are Seagrass or Jute Rugs Good With Pets?</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">Both can work, but the weave matters. Claws can catch on loops or loose fibers, and urine accidents are harder to deal with on plant fibers because you have to limit moisture while still removing the odor (3). If your pet has frequent accidents, an easy-to-wash synthetic rug is the more practical choice.</p></p>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">Which Is Better, Sisal or Seagrass?</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">It depends on your priorities. Sisal comes from the agave plant and is stiff, strong, and dyeable but rough to the touch, while seagrass is smoother and more stain-repellent but can’t really be dyed (1). Choose sisal if you want color and maximum durability, and seagrass if you want a smooth, low-maintenance surface.</p></p>
</section>

<section id="conclusion" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">Conclusion</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">Seagrass and jute both bring a natural, earthy feel to a room, but they meet different needs. Seagrass is tough, smooth, and low-maintenance, which suits dry, high-traffic living and dining areas, jute is soft and comfortable under bare feet, which suits bedrooms and quieter spaces. Whichever you choose, keep the rug dry, and check the weave and backing before you buy.</p></p>
</section>

<section id="references" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">References</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">(1) Molly in Maine. (2024, July 21). <em>Keeping it natural: A guide to jute, sisal, and seagrass rugs</em>. https://mollyinmaine.com/keeping-it-natural-a-guide-to-jute-sisal-and-seagrass-rugs/</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">(2) Seagrass-Watch. (n.d.). <em>Is it seagrass?</em> Retrieved November 12, 2026, from https://www.seagrasswatch.org/seagrassproducts/</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0">(3) Mikolajczak, L. (2024, July 15). <em>Seagrass vs. jute: What is the difference?</em> Heikoa. https://tapis-heikoa.com/en-us/blogs/tout-savoir-sur-les-tapis/quelle-est-la-difference-entre-le-jonc-de-mer-et-le-jute</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c0 c3"></p></p>
</section>`
