    contentHtml: `<section id="what-is-hemp-rope" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">What Is Hemp Rope?</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">Hemp rope is a strong, natural cordage made from the long bast fibers of the industrial hemp plant (Cannabis sativa). Workers harvest the plant stalks, extract and clean the outer fibers, and twist or braid them together into durable strands.</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c8">Although industrial hemp and marijuana belong to the same species, fiber-grade hemp varieties typically contain less than 0.3% THC, far too little to produce psychoactive effects. In contrast, medicinal and recreational cannabis varieties can contain THC concentrations ranging from 2% to over 20% (1). It's also important to distinguish industrial hemp from "Manila hemp." Despite its name, Manila rope is made from abacá, a banana plant species unrelated to cannabis(1).</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">You’ll generally come across three types sold under the name "hemp rope":</p></p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-[#2d3748] mb-4">
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Natural hemp rope:</strong> made from 100% untreated hemp fiber, with a slightly fuzzy texture and an earthy golden-brown color.</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Tarred hemp rope:</strong> hemp fiber coated in tar to keep water out, the classic cordage of sailing ships (1).</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Synthetic hemp rope:</strong> man-made fibers designed to mimic the look of real hemp, offering better resistance to sun and rain but no biodegradability (2).</li>
</ul>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">A Brief History of Hemp Rope</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">Hemp ranks among the first plants to be spun into usable fiber, possibly as far back as 50,000 years ago (1). In China, pottery from the 5th millennium BC carries hemp fiber imprints, and the Chinese later used the plant for clothing, footwear, ropes, and an early form of paper (1). One of the earliest examples of hemp rope comes from Egypt around 1500 BC (1).</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">Across Europe, farmers grew hemp mainly for its fiber to supply ship ropes, including those on the vessels of Christopher Columbus (1). During the age of sail, crews had to coat hemp rope in tar because its fibers trapped water inside the core and tended to rot, a labor-intensive job that gave sailors the nickname "Jack Tar" (1). Hemp rope was later superseded by lighter Manila rope, which doesn’t require tarring, and eventually by synthetic materials. Still, during World War II, the United States ramped up hemp farming to make rope when its Manila supply was cut off (1).</p></p>
</section>

<section id="benefits-and-drawbacks-of-hemp-rope" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">Benefits and Drawbacks of Hemp Rope</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">Hemp rope is sturdy, low-stretch, easy to grip, and eco-friendly, though it soaks up moisture, so it needs to stay dry and takes more care than synthetic rope.</p></p>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">Benefits of Hemp Rope</h3>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-[#2d3748] mb-4">
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Strength and Durability:</strong> hemp bast fibers deliver high tensile strength and low stretch, which historically made them dependable for maritime rigging, agriculture, and construction. Hemp rope outperforms cotton in strength, though nylon is generally stronger still (3).</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Natural Resistance:</strong> compared with many other plant fibers, hemp holds up better against mold, mildew, and UV rays when it’s kept in dry conditions (3).</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Biodegradability:</strong> since it comes from a plant, hemp rope breaks down naturally over time, unlike synthetic alternatives. The hemp plant also matures quickly, in about three to four months, while needing little water and few pesticides (1).</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Practical Grip and Rustic Look:</strong> Its soft yet slightly rough, fuzzy texture gives hemp rope a comfortable grip for handrails while adding a natural, rustic look (2).</li>
</ul>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">Drawbacks of Hemp Rope</h3>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-[#2d3748] mb-4">
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Moisture Sensitivity:</strong> untreated hemp can absorb water in its inner core, which can lead to rot if it isn’t dried or traditionally treated with tar. Strands that stay wet can also develop mildew, shrink, and suffer water damage (2).</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Maintenance:</strong> because moisture affects it so readily, natural hemp rope needs dry storage and more frequent checks to keep its strength and appearance (2).</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Shorter Outdoor Lifespan:</strong> outdoors, natural hemp fibers usually wear down faster than synthetic ones (2).</li>
</ul>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">Sources disagree sharply on this point: some sellers say hemp rope stands up to harsh weather without rotting (3), while others warn that it soaks up water and is prone to rot (2). That’s because twisted strands draw liquid into the core through capillary action, so the rope can look dry outside while staying damp inside (1). Natural hemp rope works best in dry spots, for damp areas or year-round outdoor use, pick tarred hemp or synthetic hemp rope.</p></p>
</section>

<section id="what-is-hemp-rope-used-for" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">What Is Hemp Rope Used For?</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">Hemp rope is mainly used for crafts and decor, agriculture and utility, and a few traditional applications.</p></p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-[#2d3748] mb-4">
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Crafts and Decor:</strong> macramé pieces and knotted jewelry (1), handfasting cords, stair handrails, and rustic home accents. Hemp is one of many natural fibers used around the house, our natural materials guide covers the other fiber groups.</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Agriculture and Utility:</strong> securing cargo, garden tasks, fencing around decking and play spaces (2), dog tug toys, and climbing ropes for fitness training (3).</li>
  <li class="leading-relaxed"><strong>Traditional Applications:</strong> sailing ropes, anchor lines, and construction ropes for bridges and door frames (1), where hemp led the way before Manila rope and synthetic materials replaced it.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section id="hemp-rope-faqs" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">Hemp Rope FAQs</h2>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">Is Hemp Rope Stronger Than Regular Rope?</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">That depends on which rope you compare it with. Hemp rope beats cotton in strength and has a higher tensile strength than jute, though nylon is generally stronger and carries heavier loads (3). Actual strength also varies with the rope’s thickness, strand count, and how the fibers are twisted or braided (3).</p></p>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">How Much Does Hemp Rope Cost?</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">Hemp rope prices vary with diameter, length, and rope type. Sources also give mixed comparisons with synthetics: one seller says hemp rope costs less than synthetic cordage (3), while another lists synthetic hemp rope as the cheaper option compared with real hemp (2). So compare prices for matching diameters and lengths before you buy.</p></p>
  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-serif text-[#14365d] mt-6 mb-3">Is Hemp Rope the Same as Jute Rope?</h3>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">No. While both come from natural bast fibers, hemp rope is made from Cannabis sativa, whereas jute rope comes from the Corchorus plant. Hemp feels softer against the skin and has a higher tensile strength, while jute has a rougher texture (3). </p></p>
</section>

<section id="conclusion" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">Conclusion</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">Hemp rope is a natural cordage made from industrial hemp bast fibers, and it was the main rope of the sailing era before Manila rope and synthetics took over. It offers solid strength, low stretch, an easy grip, and biodegradability, though it soaks up water and can rot if it stays damp. Stick with natural hemp rope for indoor crafts and decor, and choose tarred or synthetic hemp rope when you need cordage for wet spots or outdoor use.</p></p>
</section>

<section id="references" class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0c2340] mb-4">References</h2>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">(1) Wikipedia contributors. (2026). Hemp. In <em>Wikipedia</em>. Retrieved November 12, 2026, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hemp</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">(2) Mason, E. (2025, January 30). <em>Synthetic hemp rope vs natural hemp: Which is the greatest choice for outdoor use?</em> Rope Warehouse. https://www.ropewarehouse.co.uk/blog/news/synthetic-hemp-rope-vs-natural-hemp-which-is-the-greatest-choice-for-outdoor-use/</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2">(3) Hemptique. (n.d.). <em>What is hemp rope and how is it made?</em> Retrieved November 12, 2026, from https://hemptique.com/pages/what-is-a-hemp-rope</p></p>
  <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[#2d3748] mb-4"><p class="c2 c11"></p></p>
</section>`
